.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1246 AM PDT Fri Jul 31 2026
(Today and tonight)

Near term Woodside Fire Weather: Satellite imagery shows some heat
still showing up for the Woodside Fire. Thankfully the winds have
eased from what was being observed during the evening hours.
Still seeing some occasional gusts of 10-15 mph nearby. There is
a shallow marine influence still showing up with cooler temps and
moderate to excellent humidity recover. However, go up the hill
on the east side of Hwy 1 just a few 100 feet and it`s milder and
drier. There is a little bit of northerly flow over those ridge
tops too, which is likely enhancing the thermal belt. Today`s
challenge won`t be the temps or humidity, but the winds. Similar
to Thursday NW winds will pick up again and be strongest in the
afternoon/evening. Transport flow will be NW again leading to
smoke transport to the southeast and east.

Weather highlights for Friday: The warming trend observed on
Thursday will continue today as high pressure aloft continues to
ooze westward. Per 00z model guidance 850mb temps warm an
additional 1-3C leading to 3-5F degrees at the surface. That`s
assuming a fully mixed atmosphere with no marine layer influence.
In other words, across the interior. Thursday`s hottest locations
were 100-103 so a max of 105 isn`t out of the question today.
Regardless, solid temps in the 90s to low 100s across the interior.
Those temps will translate to widespread HeatRisk of Moderate with
a few pockets of Major. At the coast and near the bays we still
see subtle onshore flow with natural AC. The temp difference
between the coast and inland will lead to gusty onshore winds this
afternoon again.

Given the nearby fire activity added smoke to the forecast with
greatest coverage over the Sonoma and Petaluma Valleys of the
North Bay. Could see some hazy condition develop over Marin and
Napa as well depending on fire activity this afternoon.

Clouds maybe lacking early this morning, but a weak cold front
will approach from the N on Friday night/Saturday morning with a
higher probability for patchy coastal stratus. Given the warm
airmass aloft expect thermal belts to be active again with only
minor relief expected in the hills.

