.LONG TERM...
Issued at 141 AM PDT Fri Jul 31 2026
(Saturday through Thursday)

The aforementioned cold front fades out during the day Saturday
with little to no fanfare. Even with the fading frontal boundary Saturday
still appears to be the hottest day for this round of heat. No
big change to previous forecast. Hottest interior locations of the
Central Coast have the highest probability of exceeding 105
degrees, but less than 10% of reaching 110. Still expecting
widespread HeatRisk of Moderate with a few pockets of Major. At
the coast/bays temperatures will remain closer to seasonal
levels. The large upper level ridge gets buffeted by a passing
upper level trough to the north on Sunday. This will pause any
additional warming with moderating temperatures to round out the
weekend. Cooling 850mb and lower 500mb heights will bring subtle
cooling Monday through the middle of next week. Do want to point
out that even thought we technically cool next week this will be
a prolonged period with max temps in the 90s to lower 100s across
the interior.

As discussed last night it still looks dangerously windy over the
northern coastal waters this weekend, especially Sunday into
Monday. The N-S gradient is still projected to reach 8 to 9mb
resulting in storm force gusts. The ECMWF EFI has doubled down on
tonight`s run and is showing decent confidence for the windy
scenario. Thankfully the flow remains mostly parallel to the
coast so a wind advisory win`t be needed. The passing system to
the north will help to set the stage for bursts of offshore flow
Saturday and Sunday nights. The setup isn`t ideal for strong
winds, but still a change to NE wind. The NE flow will enhance
fire weather concerns. More details below.

