.BEACHES...
Issued at 1216 PM PDT Thu Jul 30 2026

Long period southerly to southwesterly swell of 14-17 seconds
continues to impact our coastline through at least Saturday
afternoon, bringing hazardous beach conditions to the Central
Coast and the San Francisco Bay Area. Sneaker waves and strong rip
currents will be the primary threats, with breaking waves up to
10 feet along some south and southwest facing beaches and break
points, such as Santa Cruz, Pacifica, and parts of Stinson or
Ocean Beach. This can be particularly surprising when wave heights
increase suddenly after a period of seemingly tranquil wave
action. In fact, sneaker waves can have 20+ minutes in between the
larger sets (breaking waves) with significant "flat/calm looking"
times between them. Due to the long period, sneaker waves are
powerful and can catch beachgoers by surprise, resulting in people
and pets being swept into the cold, turbulent ocean. Rip currents
are strong enough to pull even the strongest swimmers away from
shore. Check marine conditions before you head out! Swim near a
lifeguard, if possible. Maintain a safe distance from the water,
and stay away from jetties, piers, beachside rocks, and other
waterside infrastructure. Never turn your back to the ocean and if
you have small children or pets at the beach, please keep an eye
on them.

